    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a set of walnut cabinet carcasses I'm working on. I'm cutting through dovetails, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the tips of the pins every time I finish the cut. The wood is quite stable, so I don't think it's a moisture issue. Any ideas on what might be causing this or how to fix my setup?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The splintering you're experiencing at the tips of the pins is likely due to the cutting technique or tool. Using high-speed cutting with a sharp blade, particularly dovetail jigsaws or router bits, can create excessive heat, which can crack or split the wood. Consider reducing frequency cutter contact, using a stabilizing pin or end grain glue to help absorb the shock, or practicing cuts on scrap wood to optimize your technique. A chisel set, especially broad, curved, and filtered chisels, can also help to smooth the split marks.",
      "source": "target",
