    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the advice. I've already checked my chisel edges and they're razor sharp, but maybe the angle is off. I've also been trying to be extra careful about ensuring I'm cutting with the grain, but even then, the very tip of the pin seems to just give way. Do you think it could be the way the fibers are reacting to the pressure of the chisel, or is there a specific way I should be bracing the wood to prevent that blowout at the end of the stroke?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The way the wood fibers are reacting to the pressure can indeed play a role in causing the blowout at the tip of the pin. It's likely due to weak spots in the wood, often caused by the marquetry or cross-grain cuts that occur during the dovetail cutting process. To alleviate this, try bracing the wood slightly with a thin shim or a piece of scrap wood underneath the tension side of the joint. This will help to distribute the force of the chisel and reduce the pressure on the already weakened fibers at the tip of the pin.",
      "source": "target",
