      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-quality hardwoods like walnut can indeed exhibit unpredictable behavior, particularly when being mortised and tenoned. One common phenomenon is a slight play in the \"pressure angle\" of the wood, where the fibers are resisting the cutting action by a degree more than the grain pattern suggests. This can be more pronounced in hardwoods with a stronger fiber orientation, like walnut. Another possibility is that the wood has a small, unintended grain angle within that you're cutting with, resulting in inconsistent resistance at the joint ends.",
